  "summary": "The village of Pipili, Puri district, Odisha, India, is well known for its appliqué work, traditionally known as Chandua (Odia: ଚାନ୍ଦୁଆ Cānduā) in India.",
  "description": "The village of Pipili, Puri district, Odisha, India, is well known for its appliqué work, traditionally known as Chandua (Odia: ଚାନ୍ଦୁଆ Cānduā) in India. \"Appliqué\" comes from the French word appliquer, meaning \"to put on\". There are two variants to this technique: appliqué, where a fabric shape is sewn over a base layer, and reverse appliqué, wherein two layers of fabric are laid down, and a shape is subsequently cut out from the upper layer, exposing the lower layer, before both are stitched together.\n\nIt is one of the products which has been granted Geographical Indication (GI) by the government of India (Application No. 86 and 108).",
  "culturalContext": "Registered as a Geographical Indication of India (handicraft, Odisha; registration 2008–09), which protects the name for products made in its place of origin by traditional methods.",
